rpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Detailed rationale:**\\n\\nThe redesign consolidates three inconsistent conflict models into one (local metadata authoritative, sections union, per-entry struck-wins then longer-content-wins) and applies it uniformly across all sync paths. This directly reinforces the harness's Design Principle: \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff)\\\" \u2014 by making the backlog cache state deterministic and replicable, not dependent on which sync path runs first.\\n\\nThe write-through pattern with per-item watermarks and dirty-flag protection is consistent with the harness's established `GistTaskLayer` precedent (CLAUDE.md lines 562\u2013566), which implements the same pattern for SAM task plans: write local first, read-check the remote watermark, then push if unchanged. The item description explicitly confirms this parallelism: \\\"GistTaskLayer \u2014 Write-Through Plan Storage\\\" is the model the redesign applies to the backlog sync layer.\\n\\nThe redesign maintains the `BacklogBackend` Protocol contract by adding backend-gating checks (no-op under non-GitHub backends) without breaking signature compatibility. Existing implementations (`sqlite_backend`, `memory_backend`, `beads_backend`) remain valid; the GitHub backend gains enhanced capabilities (watermark comparison, dirty-flag guard, bounded timeouts) without forcing non-optional methods on other backends. This respects the Protocol's pluggable abstraction principle.\\n\\nThe offline-first semantics align with the harness's stated \\\"Fallback behavior\\\" under `ArtifactBackend Protocol` (CLAUDE.md lines 614\u2013616): when remote is unavailable, fall back to local. The redesign extends this pattern to the backlog cache itself \u2014 offline reads return local, offline writes set dirty, background sync skips when offline.\\n\\nHistorical direction: Commit 8891c8c8 (\\\"perf(backlog-sync): bound GitHub sync with shared fetch, batched mutations, and offline short-circuit\\\") is the immediate stopgap that this full redesign supersedes. The item description acknowledges this: \\\"the immediate 10-minute-hang mitigation... is being applied separately; this item is the full offline-first redesign that supersedes that stopgap.\\\" This demonstrates continuous refinement toward the complete solution \u2014 not a reversal of prior merged work, but an elevation of it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **redesign backlog github sync: offline-first with per-item watermarks, awaited write-through + dirty/outbox, and one consistent conflict model** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session design discussion 2026-06-04\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-04\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:e9ccae40efd2ff7a2972bcca52d57804 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-04)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nEffort: High \u2014 10+ files, new dirty/watermark model, outbox design sub-decision, new test suite required. See Effort section for full breakdown.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n**Blocks:**\\n- Safe multi-session use of the MCP backlog server: any session that writes and then restarts the server risks losing those writes to background overwrite.\\n- Offline and CI use under non-GitHub backends (sqlite, memory, beads): the background sync loop runs unconditionally and makes live GitHub API calls, failing or polluting network state even in test environments.\\n- The grooming agent pipeline: agents that write via `backlog_groom` and then wait for a subsequent session have no guarantee their writes survive the next server startup.\\n\\n**Bottleneck:**\\n- Multi-minute MCP server freeze during startup sync on repositories with 100+ open issues, blocking all backlog tool calls for the freeze duration.\\n- Data loss is silent: no error, no warning, no `dirty` flag visible to the caller \u2014 the write returns success, then is overwritten asynchronously.\\n- The inconsistent conflict model means which version of an item survives depends on command ordering, not user intent: `backlog_pull` merges, background sync overwrites, push overwrites GitHub. Three paths, three policies, zero consistency.\\n\\n**Who is affected:**\\n- Maintainers using the MCP backlog server across multiple sessions.\\n- CI/CD workflows running the MCP server under non-GitHub backends.\\n- Agents that write groomed content and assume durability.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Local writes are durable: a write that returns success is guaranteed to survive server restart, background sync, and offline periods \u2014 the caller's result is the actual state.\\n- No more multi-minute sync hangs: every GitHub network call is bounded by a hard timeout; the server never freezes waiting for serial GraphQL round-trips.\\n- GitHub API calls are correctly scoped: non-GitHub backends (sqlite, memory, beads) see zero GitHub API calls \u2014 offline CI and test runs no longer require network access.\\n- Conflict resolution is deterministic: one consistent merge model (`merge_item` semantics) applies across all sync paths; the outcome of concurrent edits is predictable regardless of which path runs.\\n- Incremental sync is cheap: staleness detection requires one `{number, updatedAt}` query for the full set rather than N individual fetches \u2014 sync overhead scales with the number of changed items, not the backlog size.\\n- Offline-capable: reads serve from local cache when GitHub is unavailable; writes set a dirty flag and report \\\"saved locally, pending push\\\" so the caller knows the state; reconnect flushes the outbox.\\n- Agent-safe: agents that write via `backlog_groom` can trust that a success result is durable and will be reflected in the next read, regardless of whether background sync runs between the write and the next session.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n**WRITE (working on an item):**\\n- The MCP tool returns immediately after confirming the write is locally durable.\\n- When GitHub is reachable: the tool also reports whether the write was pushed to GitHub in this call \u2014 it does not return before the push completes, but it cannot hang indefinitely (the push is hard-bounded).\\n- When GitHub is unreachable: the tool returns \\\"saved locally, pending push\\\" \u2014 no network call is attempted.\\n- A write that returns success is always locally durable. It will not be overwritten by a subsequent background sync until the pending push has been acknowledged by GitHub.\\n\\n**READ (single item):**\\n- When GitHub is reachable: the MCP tool returns the locally cached version if the remote has not changed since the last acknowledged sync. If the remote has changed, the tool fetches, merges if necessary, and returns the merged result.\\n- When GitHub is unreachable: the tool returns the local version.\\n- A read never silently returns stale data when the remote has moved and the connection is available.\\n\\n**BACKGROUND reconcile:**\\n- When the selected backend is not GitHub (sqlite, memory, beads): no background sync task starts. The server makes zero GitHub API calls during its lifespan.\\n- When the selected backend is GitHub: background sync runs incrementally \u2014 it identifies which items have changed remotely using a single bulk query, then pulls only those that have diverged and are not locally pending. It does not overwrite items that have pending local writes.\\n- Background sync does not run concurrently with foreground writes.\\n\\n**CONFLICT (both local and remote changed since last acknowledged sync):**\\n- The merge outcome follows `merge_item` rules: local metadata is authoritative; body sections are unioned; per-entry, the struck entry wins; for entries of equal struck state, longer content wins; per groomed subsection, longer content wins.\\n- This rule applies uniformly across all code paths \u2014 `backlog_pull`, background sync, and foreground write \u2014 so the outcome is the same regardless of which path triggers the merge.\\n\\n**LIFECYCLE divergence:**\\n- The following cases require a defined rule (not yet resolved in the spec \u2014 each case must have a confirmed outcome before implementation):\\n  - Issue closed remotely, item active locally.\\n  - Item resolved locally, issue open remotely.\\n  - Issue deleted remotely.\\n- The correct outcome for each of these cases is to be confirmed with the human owner before implementation.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. **Watermark comparison uses remote-vs-stored, never local clock** \u2014 no comparison path uses `datetime.now()` or `time.time()` against a remote timestamp. Testable: grep for `datetime.now()` and `time.time()` in watermark comparison code paths returns zero matches.\\n\\n2. **Every GitHub network call has a hard timeout** \u2014 no GitHub API call can block indefinitely. Testable: grep for timeout enforcement on all `gh_client` call sites in the sync layer; absence of any unguarded call is the pass criterion.\\n\\n3. **Dirty item is never overwritten by background reconcile until pushed** \u2014 a locally modified item with a pending-push flag is not overwritten by the background sync loop. Testable: concurrent write + background reconcile test where the dirty item's local content survives the reconcile cycle.\\n\\n4. **Background reconcile does not run under non-GitHub backends** \u2014 when the selected backend is sqlite, memory, or beads, zero GitHub API calls are made during server lifespan. Testable: mock backend type check; assert no network call is initiated when backend is non-GitHub.\\n\\n5. **Local write succeeds offline** \u2014 when GitHub is unreachable, a `backlog_groom` call returns a success result with text \\\"saved locally, pending push\\\" and makes zero network calls. Testable: offline write test with mocked network unavailability.\\n\\n6. **On push success, stored watermark equals returned `updatedAt`** \u2014 after a successful write-through push, the per-item stored watermark matches the `updatedAt` value returned by the `updateIssue` mutation response. Testable: mock mutation response with a known `updatedAt` value; assert stored watermark equals that value.\\n\\n7. **Lifecycle divergence rules produce the correct outcome for all cases** \u2014 the three divergence cases (issue closed remotely while item active locally; item resolved locally while issue open remotely; issue deleted remotely) each produce the defined outcome. Testable: unit test per case against the confirmed rule. Note: the confirmed outcome for each case must be established before these tests can be written (see Questions for Human).\\n\\n8. **`updateIssue` mutation response includes `updatedAt`** \u2014 the GraphQL mutation used for write-through push returns `updatedAt` in its selection set, enabling watermark capture without a separate fetch. Testable: inspect the mutation response shape; assert `updatedAt` is present in the returned node.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Discovery artifact | Feature context gist: `e9ccae40efd2ff7a2972bcca52d57804` (referenced in item Context section as `artifact-gist`) |\\n| Stopgap commit | `8891c8c8` \u2014 \\\"perf(backlog-sync): bound GitHub sync with shared fetch, batched mutations, and offline short-circuit\\\" \u2014 the immediate hang mitigation this redesign supersedes |\\n| Research finding | `updateIssue` mutation gap: `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` in `gh_client.py` lines 65-73 does not select `updatedAt` in response \u2014 watermark capture requires mutation expansion or a post-push fetch |\\n| Research finding | `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields confirmed in `BacklogItemMetadata` at lines 718-719 of `models.py`; accessible via `item.metadata.updated_at` |\\n| Research finding | No outbox model exists in the codebase (grep confirmed zero matches for \\\"outbox\\\"); no write-failure retry pattern exists for foreground calls |\\n| Cross-module boundary | `sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py` calls `_update_issue_graphql` at lines 411 and 446 and reads `node[\\\"updatedAt\\\"]` at line 166 \u2014 must be coordinated with any signature change |\\n| Prior work | `GistTaskLayer` write-through pattern in `CLAUDE.md` lines 562-566 \u2014 established precedent in the same harness for local-first write + watermark read + bounded push |\\n| Codebase | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/` \u2014 sync layer source |\\n| Tests | `plugins/development-harness/tests/` \u2014 test suite covering the affected modules |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n**Depends on:**\\n- Stopgap commit `8891c8c8` is in the dependency chain but is **superseded** by this work: the bounded-sync and batched-mutation work from the stopgap is absorbed into the full redesign. The stopgap code in the sync paths will be replaced, not built upon. Coordinators should treat the stopgap as the current-state baseline, not as scaffolding to preserve.\\n\\n**Coordination required (not a blocker, but must be verified):**\\n- `sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py` consumes `_update_issue_graphql` at the module boundary. If the signature of that function changes as part of this redesign, the SAM subsystem consumer breaks. Coordination must happen during implementation: either the internal expansion keeps the public signature stable, or `github_task.py` is updated in the same change.\\n\\n**Blocks:**\\n- Safe multi-session grooming agent workflows that rely on `backlog_groom` write durability.\\n- Any future work that adds new sync capabilities to the backlog server \u2014 the current inconsistent model must be resolved before adding to it.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n**High.**\\n\\n**Scope:** The change touches the BacklogItem model, the GitHub GraphQL client, three distinct sync entry points in `operations.py`, the backend protocol, the GitHub backend implementation, three other backends (no-op guards), `sync_engine.py`, and `server.py`. The cross-module consumer (`github_task.py`) requires coordinated verification. That is 10+ files with behavioral changes across the sync layer.\\n\\n**Tests required:** New unit tests are needed for: offline write behavior, watermark comparison logic, dirty-flag guard against background overwrite, backend-type gating, each lifecycle divergence rule outcome, mutation response shape, and concurrent write + reconcile interaction. Existing tests in `test_backlog_core_github.py`, `test_graphql_helpers.py`, and `test_live_validation.py` will need updates to match the new sync contract.\\n\\n**Pre-implementation design sub-decisions required (both must be resolved before a task can be written for the relevant area):**\\n\\n1. **Outbox retry policy** (RT-ICA MISSING condition): cadence (background reconcile cycle vs explicit retry command vs both), driver, and persistence format (in-memory queue, YAML metadata field, or separate file). No existing pattern in the codebase.\\n\\n2. **Lifecycle divergence outcomes**: the three divergence cases (issue closed remotely vs active locally; item resolved locally vs open remotely; issue deleted remotely) are listed in the spec as \\\"define the rule\\\" \u2014 the correct outcome for each case must be confirmed with the human owner before unit tests for AC #7 can be written.\\n\\n### API State\\n\\n**BacklogItem model** (models.py lines 858-1010):\\n- `title: str`, `description: str` \u2014 primary content fields\\n- `metadata: BacklogItemMetadata` \u2014 persisted to YAML frontmatter\\n- `sections: dict[str, Section | GroomedData]` \u2014 section content, entry-block keyed\\n\\n**BacklogItemMetadata** (models.py lines 704-730):\\n- `last_synced: str = \\\"\\\"` \u2014 ISO 8601 timestamp field, persisted to YAML (line 718). [CROSS-ANGLE] NOT set automatically by the sync engine \u2014 no assignment found in any sync code path.\\n- `updated_at: str = \\\"\\\"` \u2014 persisted to YAML (line 719). Set by `issue_to_local_fields()` from GitHub's `issue[\\\"updatedAt\\\"]` on pull. This IS the GitHub-side coarse timestamp.\\n- [CROSS-ANGLE] No `dirty` flag, no `outbox_pending`, no per-section watermark fields exist on the model.\\n\\n**GraphQL mutation response \u2014 critical gap:**\\nThe `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` (gh_client.py lines 218-230) returns only `{ id, number, state }`. [CROSS-ANGLE] `updatedAt` is NOT in the response selection set and is not capturable from the current mutation. `_update_issue_graphql` returns `None` \u2014 the full mutation response is discarded (gh_client.py line 776). Any watermark based on write-time requires a separate post-write fetch or expanding the mutation selection set.\\n\\n**sync_issues_graphql** (gh_client.py lines 629-701):\\n- Reads `.last_sync` file before fetch (when `track_timestamp=True`)\\n- Records sync-start timestamp BEFORE fetching \u2014 documented race-avoidance (line 685 comment)\\n- `filterBy.since` uses ISO 8601 timestamp for incremental fetch\\n\\n**refresh_local_cache_from_github** (operations.py lines 2136-2220):\\n- Incremental mode: reads `.last_sync` \u2192 `_sync_incremental` (single GraphQL fetch, OPEN+CLOSED since timestamp)\\n- Full mode: `_sync_full` (two-pass: OPEN then CLOSED)\\n- No write-through: groom tool call writes locally only; push is batch-only via `sync_items`\\n\\n**sync_items** (operations.py lines 3620-3668):\\n- Single GitHub connection, single GraphQL fetch shared across both passes\\n- Pass 1: `sync_create_missing_issues`; Pass 2: `sync_push_groomed_content`\\n- No outbox; no dirty flag; push is explicit pull-then-push, not event-driven\\n\\n**merge_item** (github_sync.py lines 403-470):\\n- Local metadata fields win unconditionally (title, priority, status, issue, source, plan)\\n- Section content: union merge by entry ID \u2014 entries only in remote are added, entries only in local are kept\\n- GroomedData: per-subsection longer-content-wins (no timestamp comparison)\\n- No last-write-wins by timestamp; no conflict model for metadata fields\\n\\n**Timeout patterns** (gh_client.py):\\n- `get_github()`: `timeout=15` \u2192 PyGithub `Github(timeout=...)` (line 1181)\\n- `try_get_github()`: hardcoded `timeout=10` (line 1199)\\n- [CROSS-ANGLE] No `asyncio.timeout()` wrapping `asyncio.to_thread()` calls; MCP transport enforces 60s tool deadline (gh_client.py line 1166-1167)\\n\\n### Codebase Audit\\n\\n**BacklogItem and BacklogItemMetadata \u2014 sync-relevant fields:**\\n\\n`last_synced: str = \\\"\\\"` \u2014 exists at models.py lines 718 and 903. [CROSS-ANGLE] No code path sets this field during `refresh_local_cache_from_github`, `sync_push_groomed_content`, or any other sync function. Present but unused by the engine.\\n\\n`updated_at: str = \\\"\\\"` \u2014 models.py line 719. Set via `issue_to_local_fields()` (gh_client.py line 1713) from GitHub's `issue[\\\"updatedAt\\\"]` on pull. Coarse whole-item timestamp \u2014 bumps on label/comment/state changes, not section-specific.\\n\\n**_startup_sync_loop \u2014 behavioral contract:**\\n\\n- Entry: called once from FastMCP lifespan hook (server.py line 1566). Gated by `_startup_sync_enabled()` AND `state.try_start()`.\\n- `try_start()` is synchronous check-and-set \u2014 race-free under single-threaded event loop (sync_state.py lines 117-133).\\n- Configured via `backlog.startup_sync.enabled` in `.dh/config.yaml`.\\n- Retry policy: 3 attempts, delays `(30.0, 120.0)` seconds. Retry sleep is OUTSIDE the lock.\\n- Termination: SUCCESS \u2192 `IDLE`; NON_RETRYABLE \u2192 `OFFLINE`; retries exhausted \u2192 `ERROR`.\\n- Work dispatched as: `asyncio.to_thread(operations.refresh_local_cache_from_github, ...)`.\\n\\n**GraphQL updateIssue \u2014 confirmed no updatedAt return:**\\n\\n[CROSS-ANGLE] `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` selection set is `{ id, number, state }` only (gh_client.py lines 218-230). `updatedAt` absent. `_update_issue_graphql` returns `None` (line 776). The GitHub-assigned write timestamp is not available to the caller after a write. Expanding the mutation selection set to include `updatedAt` is a one-line change to `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` and a return-type change on `_update_issue_graphql`.\\n\\n**Async/locking primitive inventory:**\\n\\nPresent: `asyncio.Lock` (SyncState.lock), `asyncio.to_thread`, `asyncio.sleep`, `asyncio.Task`, `asyncio.create_task`, `loop.call_soon_threadsafe`.\\n\\nAbsent (available from stdlib): `asyncio.Queue`, `asyncio.Event`, `asyncio.Semaphore`.\\n\\nFor a foreground write-through + outbox: `asyncio.Queue` is the idiomatic Python serialization primitive for an event-driven work queue. Not present but requires no new dependency.\\n\\n**Per-section entry timestamps \u2014 watermark assessment:**\\n\\nEntry blocks: `<div><sub>{ISO_TIMESTAMP}</sub>\\\\n\\\\n{content}\\\\n</div>` (entry_blocks.py line 11). Entry IDs are ISO 8601 timestamps parseable to UTC-aware `datetime` via `_parse_entry_timestamp()`. `parse_entries(..., since=datetime)` filters by entry timestamp (entry_blocks.py line 169).\\n\\nPer-section entry timestamps ARE usable as fine-grained watermarks at the section-write level. More precise than whole-item `updatedAt`. Limitation: entry timestamps reflect local write time, not GitHub write time \u2014 these diverge during offline periods.\\n\\n**merge_item \u2014 conflict model:**\\n\\nLocal metadata is unconditionally authoritative (github_sync.py lines 403-470). Section content is additive (union by entry ID). No timestamp-based last-write-wins for metadata. The proposed redesign requires extending this contract to handle remote-authoritative fields written-through from a different session.\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\nNone detected. All four angles produced consistent findings.\\n\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\n\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `updateIssue` mutation does NOT return `updatedAt` \u2014 watermark requires mutation expansion or follow-up fetch | API State + Codebase Audit | High |\\n| `last_synced` field exists on model but is never set by sync engine | API State + Codebase Audit | High |\\n| No dirty flag, no outbox, no write-through exist in current implementation | API State + Codebase Audit | High |\\n| `asyncio.to_thread` is the established pattern for blocking GitHub I/O | Ecosystem + Codebase Audit | High |\\n| `asyncio.Lock` already present in `SyncState` for serializing sync workers | Ecosystem + Codebase Audit | High |\\n| No performance benchmarks exist for the sync layer | Impact + Ecosystem | Medium |\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\n**Established async patterns in this codebase:**\\n\\n[CROSS-ANGLE] `asyncio.to_thread()` is the canonical pattern for all blocking GitHub I/O \u2014 confirmed in sync_engine.py (line 109), artifact_provider.py, and dispatch_state.py. This is the integration point for foreground write-through calls.\\n\\n[CROSS-ANGLE] `asyncio.Lock` already exists in `SyncState.lock` (sync_state.py line 95) to serialize background sync workers. The lock is acquired per-attempt (`async with state.lock` in sync_engine.py line 157) and released between retries.\\n\\n**asyncio.Lock lazy-init constraint** (sync_state.py lines 7-11): `asyncio.Lock()` must be created inside a running event loop \u2014 never at module import time. Any new lock for write serialization must follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**Backoff sleep MUST be outside the lock** (sync_engine.py line 227 comment): \\\"Sleep outside the lock so other tool calls can proceed during the wait.\\\" Backoff sleeps in any retry mechanism must NOT hold the lock.\\n\\n**Progress callback thread-safety** (sync_engine.py lines 62-89): When calling into blocking functions via `asyncio.to_thread`, state mutations from the worker thread must use `loop.call_soon_threadsafe` \u2014 direct mutation is unsafe.\\n\\n**Established retry convention** (sync_engine.py lines 42-45): `MAX_RETRIES = 3`, backoff delays `(30.0, 120.0)` seconds. This is the codebase-standard retry cadence for retryable GitHub errors.\\n\\n**No asyncio.Queue, asyncio.Event, or asyncio.Semaphore** exist in the current implementation \u2014 all absent from backlog_core. Available from stdlib, compatible with FastMCP async context.\\n\\n**Foreground write outbox**: Currently absent. Foreground `backlog_groom` writes are local-only; GitHub push only happens when `sync_items` is called explicitly. No event-driven push, no pending-write queue, no dirty flag.\\n\\n**Single-writer constraint**: CLAUDE.md documents that `append_task` is NOT safe under concurrent writers \u2014 the same constraint applies to any new file-based outbox.\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n**Primary implementation files (from Impact Radius):**\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 BacklogItem and BacklogItemMetadata model definitions; existing `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields confirmed at lines 718-719.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` \u2014 GraphQL client; `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` selection set (lines 65-73) confirmed to not return `updatedAt`; all GitHub API call sites requiring timeout enforcement.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 Sync entry points: `refresh_local_cache_from_github` (background overwrite path), `sync_push_groomed_content` (push path), `refresh_pull_item` (the one path that correctly calls `merge_item_models` at line 436, line 1533).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` \u2014 Primary BacklogBackend implementor; owns the complete sync surface (HIGH risk).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` \u2014 Must remain inert for sync operations (MEDIUM risk).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py` \u2014 Local-only backend; must no-op new sync methods (MEDIUM risk).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py` \u2014 In-memory test double; must no-op new sync methods (LOW risk).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 BacklogBackend Protocol; any new sync capability method definitions appear here.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_engine.py` \u2014 Background sync task lifecycle; `_startup_sync_enabled()` confirmed to check only config YAML (lines 1488-1500 of server.py), not backend type.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `_backlog_lifespan` launches `_startup_sync_loop`; `backlog_sync`, `backlog_pull`, `api_state` MCP tool definitions.\\n\\n**Cross-module boundary (coordination required):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py` \u2014 Calls `_update_issue_graphql` at lines 411 and 446; reads `node[\\\"updatedAt\\\"]` at line 166. Signature changes to `_update_issue_graphql` break this consumer.\\n\\n**Test files (primary affected):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_github.py`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_graphql_helpers.py`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/conftest.py`\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\n- **`updateIssue` mutation does not return `updatedAt`** \u2014 watermark capture requires either expanding `_UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION` selection set (one-line change) or a separate post-write `_fetch_issue_graphql` call. Neither is implemented. (API State + Codebase Audit)\\n- **No performance benchmarks** for sync layer throughput, latency, or retry behavior \u2014 no measured data exists to baseline the redesign's performance impact. (Impact + Ecosystem)\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- Whether `.last_sync` file write is atomic (temp-file + rename) or direct `write_text` \u2014 currently `write_text` per Codebase Audit; crash safety not confirmed as known bug vs accepted risk. (Codebase Audit)\\n- Whether `last_synced` is set anywhere in the pull path (`_pull_item_update_yaml`, `save_item`, `yaml_io.py`) \u2014 yaml_io.py was not read; assignment may exist there. (Codebase Audit)\\n- Established retry cadence for a foreground write outbox \u2014 the `sync_engine` backoff `(30s, 120s)` covers background pull-sync only; no pattern exists for immediate write-failure retry in foreground calls. (Ecosystem)\\n- Pre-implementation delta sizes are approximations \u2014 actual module sizes depend on design choices not yet made. (Impact)\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: The item describes three traceable failures with identifiable root causes and reproducible conditions: (1) silent data loss via background overwrite without merge, (2) ~10-minute sync hang from serial GraphQL calls, (3) inconsistent conflict model across code paths. All three failures stem from the sync layer being built without write-through model, watermarks, dirty tracking, or backend gating \u2014 observable design gaps in the code.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys via code inspection and reproduction path identification\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Local edits are silently clobbered by background sync; concurrent remote edits are lost on push; sync operations hang for 10+ minutes \u2192 Background sync respects dirty flags and watermarks; conflicting edits merge deterministically; all GitHub calls are bounded by timeout and batched\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\n**Existing sync layer footprint** (all measurements from `wc -c`):\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. Tokens (chars\u00f73.5) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| operations.py | 233,932 | 66,838 |\\n| gh_client.py | 73,102 | 20,886 |\\n| backend_protocol.py | 38,348 | 10,957 |\\n| backends/sqlite_backend.py | 41,762 | 11,932 |\\n| parsing.py | 47,267 | 13,505 |\\n| sync_engine.py | 9,526 | 2,722 |\\n| sync_state.py | 10,228 | 2,922 |\\n| github_sync.py | 16,488 | 4,711 |\\n| entry_blocks.py | 11,684 | 3,338 |\\n| backends/beads_backend.py | 30,084 | 8,595 |\\n| backends/github_backend.py | 21,058 | 6,017 |\\n| backends/memory_backend.py | 24,181 | 6,909 |\\n| **Total** | **557,660** | **159,331** |\\n\\n**Agent editing surface**: operations.py at ~66,838 estimated tokens is **Blocking risk** for full-file reads. All edits to operations.py require targeted grep/line-range access. sync_engine.py (2,722), sync_state.py (2,922), and github_sync.py (4,711) fit comfortably in context.\\n\\n**Pre-implementation delta estimates** (constructs do not yet exist \u2014 estimates only):\\n\\n| Addition | Est. Size (chars) | Est. Tokens | Tier |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Dirty/outbox model fields (2-3 fields) | ~200 | ~57 | Negligible |\\n| Per-item watermark field | ~100 | ~29 | Negligible |\\n| Outbox queue module (new file) | ~3,000\u20135,000 | ~857\u20131,429 | Notable |\\n| Conflict model additions to merge_item | ~500 | ~143 | Negligible |\\n\\nNo benchmarks found in any sync layer file or ARCHITECTURE.md.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Run the MCP backlog server with BACKLOG_BACKEND=sqlite (or beads, or memory).\\n2. Observe `api_state` tool response \u2014 note that a background GitHub sync task starts and makes GitHub API calls despite a non-GitHub backend being selected.\\n3. Run the MCP backlog server with BACKLOG_BACKEND=github (normal config). Edit a backlog item via `backlog_groom` (write a local change).\\n4. Wait for the background `_startup_sync_loop` to run \u2014 it calls `_write_issue_node_to_cache`, overwriting the local file with GitHub's current state. The locally written change is silently lost.\\n5. Call `backlog_groom` on a large number of items (e.g., all P1 items in a project) while BACKLOG_BACKEND=github and network is available.\\n6. Observe the MCP server hang for several minutes (10 minutes confirmed via faulthandler in the original session). The faulthandler stack trace shows ~105 serial GraphQL `updateIssue` POSTs each completing HTTP 200 but accumulating serial latency with no pacing or timeout bound.\\n7. Call `backlog_pull` on an item that has unpushed local edits, then immediately trigger background sync \u2014 observe that the background sync path (`_write_issue_node_to_cache`) overwrites while `backlog_pull` correctly merges. The outcome depends on which runs first, not on any causal order.\\n\\n### Research\\n\\n\\n\\n### Research \u2014 backlog_core sync layer / offline-first redesign \u2014 2026-06-04\\n\\nResearch method: 4-angle independent fan-out. [CROSS-ANGLE] marks findings confirmed by 2+ independent angles. No conflicts detected.\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via code path inspection\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n\\n1. **Observed failure**: Local edits to a backlog item are silently overwritten when the background sync loop runs.\\n\\n2. **Why**: `_startup_sync_loop()` (created in server lifespan) calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github()` which calls `_write_issue_node_to_cache()` for every fetched GitHub issue. This function overwrites the local cache file with GitHub's current state via `save_item(item_with_github_data)` regardless of whether the local file has pending unpushed edits.\\n\\n3. **Why (deeper)**: There is no per-item dirty flag or version watermark. The code has `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields in the BacklogItem model, but the read path never performs a conditional check \u2014 no code path compares issue `updatedAt` to a stored watermark to avoid unnecessary overwrites.\\n\\n4. **Why (deeper)**: The three sync entry points apply inconsistent conflict strategies: `backlog_pull()` uses `merge_item()` (local metadata authoritative, sections union), background sync uses `_write_issue_node_to_cache()` (GitHub overwrites local), and `sync_push_groomed_content()` renders local and overwrites GitHub (discarding GitHub-only sections except Artifact Manifest). Without a unified model, which \\\"side wins\\\" depends on which command runs first \u2014 not on any observable version or causality.\\n\\n5. **Root cause**: The sync architecture was built without three missing primitives: (a) **write-through model** \u2014 local writes must anchor before pushing, dirty flag signals pending push, (b) **version watermarks** \u2014 per-item remote `updatedAt` stored locally to detect whether remote has diverged since last sync, (c) **backend gating** \u2014 background sync runs unconditionally in server lifespan (gated only on kill-switch + re-entry guard), not on backend type. When backend is not GitHub (sqlite/memory/beads), no watermark, push, pull, or GitHub API call should run.\\n\\n**Additional observations**:\\n\\n- The ~10-minute hang (105 serial GraphQL updateIssue calls in `sync_push_groomed_content()`) is a consequence of the same architectural gap \u2014 without batching, pacing, or timeout-bounding, serial calls accumulate to multi-minute latency.\\n- The background sync's unconditional GitHub API access (even under sqlite/memory/beads backends) violates backend abstraction \u2014 it checks backend availability only via a re-entry guard, never via backend type.\\n\\n**Root cause**: Sync layer lacks write-through commit model (dirty flag + watermark) and backend-gated operation control. Local edits have no protection from concurrent overwrites; remote changes have no efficient staleness detection; GitHub API access is not gated by backend type.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: \\n- `models.py`: Add `dirty: bool` and `watermark_updated_at: str | None` fields to BacklogItem\\n- `operations.py`: Modify `refresh_local_cache_from_github()` to compare `issue.updated_at` to `item.watermark_updated_at` before overwrite; add backend gate check\\n- `operations.py`: Modify sync_push_groomed_content() to batch mutations and add hard timeout bound\\n- `sync_engine.py`: Add backend type check before creating _startup_sync_loop; modify to skip runs when backend is not GitHub\\n\\n## Fact-Check Results\\n\\n<div><sub>{now_iso()}</sub>\\n\\n\\\\n\\\\n{content}\\\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scenarios The Model Must Handle\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Timestamp comparison must be remote-current-updatedAt vs the stored remote watermark \u2014 never a local file mtime vs a remote timestamp. Never compare a local clock value to a remote clock value.\\n- updatedAt is coarse (label/comment/assignee/project changes bump it) \u2014 a difference triggers reconcile even when the body did not change.\\n- The dirty flag must protect a local (unpushed) item from being overwritten by a pull/background reconcile until it is pushed.\\n- Safe write order and partial-failure handling: the local durable write (with dirty) anchors the push; never a state where an edit exists only on GitHub or is lost.\\n- Do NOT rely on filesystem mtime as the \\\"locally edited\\\" signal (git checkout / copies / hook stashing reset it) \u2014 use the explicit dirty flag.\\n- Knowing which cached items are stale must be cheap (one {number, updatedAt} query for the set), not N calls.\\n- GitHub's search index lags the issues themselves \u2014 search-tier results may be staler than a direct fetch.\\n- Offline item creation: an item created offline has no issue number -> dirty -> create + link the issue on reconnect.\\n- Closure/deletion divergence (issue closed remotely vs active locally; resolved locally vs open remotely; issue deleted remotely) \u2014 define the rule.\\n- All GitHub requests must be bounded by a timeout so an awaited push/read or background reconcile cannot hang (root of the observed 10-minute hang).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence Context This Session\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- find-cause: faulthandler caught the e2e hang in serial GraphQL `updateIssue` within `sync_push_groomed_content`; ~105 GraphQL POSTs, all 200, zero pacing sleeps, zero retries \u2014 cumulative serial round-trips, not a single stuck call.\\n- `merge_item` is wired only into the YAML pull path; the background sync uses `_overwrite_body_from_github`; the background `_startup_sync_loop` is created in the server lifespan gated only on a kill-switch + re-entry guard, not on backend.\\n- The item model already has `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields (the watermark slots), but no dirty/outbox and no conditional read.\\n\\nNote: the immediate 10-minute-hang mitigation (shared single fetch + batched aliased mutations + dry-run/offline short-circuit) is being applied separately; this item is the full offline-first redesign that supersedes that stopgap.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Claim | Verdict | Evidence |\\n|-------|---------|----------|\\n| 1. Background sync runs regardless of backend | VERIFIED | No backend check in _startup_sync_enabled() or lifespan setup |\\n| 2. merge_item wired only to YAML pull, not background sync | VERIFIED | merge_item only in refresh_pull_item; background sync uses _overwrite_body_from_github |\\n| 3. BacklogItem has last_synced and updated_at fields | VERIFIED (with note) | last_synced is direct field; updated_at is in BacklogItemMetadata |\\n| 4. _UPDATE_ISSUE_MUTATION omits updatedAt | VERIFIED | Response shape is `{ id number state }` only |\\n| 5. No dirty/outbox/write-through | VERIFIED | Zero occurrences in codebase; no such fields in models |\\n| 6. Entry timestamps are ISO 8601 UTC | VERIFIED | Regex and parsing confirm ISO 8601 with Z terminator |\\n\\n**Overall Assessment:** The Wave 0 research findings are accurate. The claims describe genuine architectural issues that require the offline-first redesign outlined in the item description.\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1560-1571 (accessed 2026-06-04)\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:436, 1533, 2073-2075, 2123, 4373-4451 (accessed 2026-06-04)\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:704-733, 858-907 (accessed 2026-06-04)\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py:65-73 (accessed 2026-06-04)\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/entry_blocks.py:9-36 (accessed 2026-06-04)\\n  VERIFIED_BY: Read + Bash grep on 2026-06-04\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem (Observed This Session)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe backlog GitHub-sync conflict model is inconsistent across entry points; which side \\\"wins\\\" on concurrent edits depends on which command runs:\\n- Explicit `backlog_pull` (YAML items) does a real field/section merge (`merge_item`): local metadata authoritative, sections union, per-entry struck-wins then longer-content-wins.\\n- The background startup sync (`refresh_local_cache_from_github` -> `_write_issue_node_to_cache`) OVERWRITES local body + metadata from GitHub.\\n- Push (`sync_push_groomed_content` -> `render_issue_body`) OVERWRITES the GitHub body from local, discarding GitHub-only sections except the Artifact Manifest.\\n\\nAdditional defects surfaced:\\n- The background GitHub sync runs regardless of the selected backend (gated only on a kill-switch + re-entry guard in the server lifespan, not on backend), so it hits the GitHub API even under sqlite/memory/beads backends.\\n- A ~10-minute hang in the e2e live sync: `sync_items` did two full-repo OPEN-issue fetches and `sync_push_groomed_content` issued one serial GraphQL `updateIssue` per item. faulthandler confirmed ~100+ serial GraphQL round-trips (105 POSTs, all HTTP 200, zero pacing sleeps, zero retries/rate-limit) \u2014 cumulative serial latency, not a single stuck call.\\n- No per-item version watermark, no dirty/pending-push flag, no outbox. The model already has `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields, but the read path never does a conditional check.\\n\\nImpact: silent data loss (local edits clobbered by the background overwrite; GitHub edits clobbered by push), a multi-minute hang on sync, and GitHub API calls under non-GitHub backends.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence / Context (This Session)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- find-cause: faulthandler caught the e2e hang in serial GraphQL `updateIssue` within `sync_push_groomed_content`; ~105 GraphQL POSTs, all 200, zero pacing sleeps, zero retries \u2014 cumulative serial round-trips, not a single stuck call.\\n- `merge_item` is wired only into the YAML pull path; the background sync uses `_overwrite_body_from_github`; the background `_startup_sync_loop` is created in the server lifespan gated only on a kill-switch + re-entry guard, not on backend.\\n- The item model already has `last_synced` and `updated_at` fields (the watermark slots), but no dirty/outbox and no conditional read.\\n\\nNote: the immediate 10-minute-hang mitigation (shared single fetch + batched aliased mutations + dry-run/offline short-circuit) is being applied separately; this item is the full offline-first redesign that supersedes that stopgap.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Desired Behavior Requirement Preserved As The Spec\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen the backend is GitHub:\\n\\nWRITE (working on an item) \u2014 awaited write-through:\\n- Write local first and set the item dirty (pending_push=true).\\n- If the backend is offline/unavailable: return early \u2014 report \\\"saved locally, pending push\\\" \u2014 do NOT attempt a watermark read or push.\\n- If healthy: re-read the issue's current updatedAt and compare to the per-item stored watermark. If it matches: await the bounded updateIssue push. If it differs (remote moved): pull + merge first, then await the bounded push of the merged result.\\n- On push success: clear dirty and store the returned updatedAt as the new watermark.\\n- On timeout/error: leave the item dirty for the outbox to retry.\\n- The push is awaited (the operation reports the real upstream result) and MUST be hard-bounded so it can never hang.\\n\\nREAD (single item):\\n- If healthy: compare the issue's current updatedAt to the per-item stored watermark. If they match, read local (no pull). If they differ, pull (merge if the item is also dirty).\\n- If offline: read local.\\n\\nLIST / SEARCH (staleness tolerated):\\n- Online: use GitHub search.\\n- For complex searches GitHub search cannot satisfy: batch-fetch {number, updatedAt} for the candidate set, refresh only the diverged stale local items, then search/grep locally.\\n\\nBACKGROUND reconcile:\\n- Watermark-incremental: one {number, updatedAt} query, then pull only the items whose remote watermark diverged AND that are not locally dirty; push dirty items first.\\n- Only runs when the selected backend is GitHub.\\n- Serialized with foreground writes (no concurrent cache writes).\\n\\nCONFLICT (both local and remote changed since the last watermark):\\n- Merge via the existing `merge_item` rules: local metadata authoritative; sections union; per-entry struck-wins then longer-content-wins; per-groomed-subsection longer-content-wins. (Confirmed as the desired behavior.)\\n\\nWhen the backend is NOT GitHub (sqlite/memory/beads): local is the source of truth \u2014 no watermark, push, pull, or background GitHub sync runs.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fact Check Results\\n\\n<div><sub>{now_iso()}</sub>\\n\\n\\\\n\\\\n{content}\\\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Desired Behavior (Requirement \u2014 Preserved As The Spec)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen the backend is GitHub:\\n\\nWRITE (working on an item) \u2014 awaited write-through:\\n- Write local first and set the item dirty (pending_push=true).\\n- If the backend is offline/unavailable: return early \u2014 report \\\"saved locally, pending push\\\" \u2014 do NOT attempt a w